# What this repository's own compose DAG loses when it is cut up by directory. One row: its cycle
# space taken whole, the sum of its cycle spaces taken one directory at a time, and whether the
# first survives the second.
#
# The sibling of `rank/decomposition.sqlc`, and the third row of a two-row table
#   That relation asks the same question of the graphs the MODEL composes, cut by filing. This
#   asks it of the graph this REPOSITORY composes, cut by directory. Same arithmetic, and the
#   verdicts differ: the layer graph survives its cut because it is a forest either way, the unit
#   graph does not because its only cycle spans two filings, and this graph does not because most
#   of its cycles span two directories.
#
# It answers a navigation question rather than raising an accusation
#   Nothing here is a defect. The compose graph's cycle space is large and free, because a query
#   is idempotent and composing a relation twice reads it once. `rank/compose_edges.sqlc` states
#   that and `rank/cycle_space.sqlc` states the opposite verdict for `pm.part`, where the same
#   shape is `checks/jagged_layer`. What the cut measures is whether the directory tree is where
#   the composition lives, and the answer is that it mostly is not.
#
# A directory is a partition, a filing is a cover
#   A template lives in exactly one directory, so `sum(n_nodes)` over the scopes equals the whole
#   graph's `n`. A layer may be filed under two documents, so the per-filing node counts in
#   `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` sum to MORE than its graph's. The two relations look alike and
#   their sums are not comparable, which `rank/compose_measures.sqlc`'s header argues.
#
# Why the sum can only fall
#   Cutting throws away every edge whose endpoints are in different directories and keeps every
#   node, so each scope's walk sees a subgraph of the whole. An edge that closed a loop across a
#   boundary closes nothing inside one, so the summed cycle space is at most the whole graph's and
#   the difference is the count of independent loops that need two directories to exist.
#
# Probe
#   Move a template into the directory of everything it composes and recompose: the summed cycle
#   space must rise, and `corpus_wide` must not move at all.
-- rank/compose_measures.sqlc at both scopes: the whole against the sum of the parts.
WITH
compose_edges AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(rank/compose_edges.sqlc)
),
compose_measures AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(rank/compose_measures.sqlc)
)
SELECT w.cycle_space_dim                                                   AS corpus_wide,
       coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint                         AS summed_per_directory,
       w.cycle_space_dim = coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint     AS survives_the_cut,
       count(p.scope)                                                      AS directories,
       w.cycle_space_dim - coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint     AS loops_that_cross
FROM      (
    :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc)
) w
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc)
) p ON p.scope IS NOT NULL
WHERE w.scope IS NULL
GROUP BY w.cycle_space_dim
